1
00:00:00,667 --> 00:00:02,657
Look at a city at night.

2
00:00:02,827 --> 00:00:07,207
Every lamp, every screen, every motor in it is running

3
00:00:07,227 --> 00:00:10,167
on electricity that was made a moment ago,

4
00:00:10,187 --> 00:00:11,537
somewhere far away.

5
00:00:12,027 --> 00:00:13,577
Follow the wires back.

6
00:00:13,707 --> 00:00:16,807
Through the substation, along the transmission line,

7
00:00:16,827 --> 00:00:18,537
into the power station.

8
00:00:18,667 --> 00:00:22,047
Whether it burns coal or gas, splits atoms,

9
00:00:22,067 --> 00:00:24,887
or stands under a dam or a wind turbine,

10
00:00:24,907 --> 00:00:27,457
at its centre you find the same thing.

11
00:00:27,787 --> 00:00:31,377
A magnet, turning, inside coils of wire.

12
00:00:31,707 --> 00:00:32,697
That is all.

13
00:00:32,827 --> 00:00:36,527
Nearly every unit of electricity you have ever used was

14
00:00:36,547 --> 00:00:39,207
made by moving a magnet near a wire.

15
00:00:39,227 --> 00:00:42,727
The principle was found in a basement laboratory in London,

16
00:00:42,747 --> 00:00:46,274
over about ten weeks in the autumn of eighteen thirty-one,

17
00:00:46,294 --> 00:00:47,807
by Michael Faraday.

18
00:00:47,827 --> 00:00:50,967
It is called electromagnetic induction.

19
00:00:50,987 --> 00:00:52,927
In this last video of the series,

20
00:00:52,947 --> 00:00:54,737
we take it apart properly.

21
00:00:56,100 --> 00:00:58,410
Last time we saw the experiment.

22
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:01,850
A steady magnetic field through a coil does nothing.

23
00:01:02,180 --> 00:01:04,730
A changing one drives a current.

24
00:01:04,900 --> 00:01:08,640
To make that exact, we need a way to say how much magnetic

25
00:01:08,660 --> 00:01:10,810
field passes through a loop of wire.

26
00:01:11,220 --> 00:01:13,130
Think of an open window in the rain.

27
00:01:13,460 --> 00:01:15,010
How much rain comes through?

28
00:01:15,140 --> 00:01:17,130
That depends on three things.

29
00:01:17,220 --> 00:01:18,890
How hard it is raining.

30
00:01:18,980 --> 00:01:20,490
How big the window is.

31
00:01:20,740 --> 00:01:24,720
And how the window is facing: tilt it edge-on to the rain,

32
00:01:24,740 --> 00:01:26,490
and almost nothing comes through.

33
00:01:26,900 --> 00:01:29,850
A loop of wire in a magnetic field is the same.

34
00:01:30,180 --> 00:01:32,960
The amount of field passing through it depends on how

35
00:01:32,980 --> 00:01:36,080
strong the field is, how large the loop is,

36
00:01:36,100 --> 00:01:37,970
and how the loop is turned.

37
00:01:38,100 --> 00:01:40,970
We call that amount the magnetic flux.

38
00:01:41,060 --> 00:01:43,320
For a loop facing the field squarely,

39
00:01:43,340 --> 00:01:46,410
it is simply the field multiplied by the area.

40
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:48,600
If you picture the field as lines,

41
00:01:48,620 --> 00:01:52,010
the flux is the number of lines passing through the loop.

42
00:01:52,100 --> 00:01:55,560
Its unit is the weber, named after Wilhelm Weber,

43
00:01:55,580 --> 00:01:58,120
whom we met in the last video measuring the constants

44
00:01:58,140 --> 00:01:59,050
of electricity.

45
00:01:59,380 --> 00:02:02,240
One tesla, spread over one square metre,

46
00:02:02,260 --> 00:02:03,530
is one weber.

47
00:02:04,900 --> 00:02:07,280
Now we can state what Faraday found,

48
00:02:07,300 --> 00:02:08,170
in one line.

49
00:02:08,660 --> 00:02:11,160
Whenever the flux through a loop changes,

50
00:02:11,180 --> 00:02:13,730
a voltage appears around the loop.

51
00:02:13,860 --> 00:02:15,920
And the faster the flux changes,

52
00:02:15,940 --> 00:02:17,370
the larger the voltage.

53
00:02:17,940 --> 00:02:21,570
Notice that it does not matter how you change the flux.

54
00:02:21,700 --> 00:02:24,400
You can make the field stronger or weaker,

55
00:02:24,420 --> 00:02:27,360
by bringing a magnet closer or switching on a current

56
00:02:27,380 --> 00:02:28,290
nearby.

57
00:02:28,420 --> 00:02:30,730
You can change the area of the loop.

58
00:02:30,820 --> 00:02:33,920
Or you can turn the loop, so that it faces the field more

59
00:02:33,940 --> 00:02:35,800
squarely, or less.

60
00:02:35,820 --> 00:02:38,400
The loop cannot tell the difference.

61
00:02:38,420 --> 00:02:40,960
Any change in flux gives a voltage.

62
00:02:40,980 --> 00:02:43,320
And it is the rate that counts.

63
00:02:43,340 --> 00:02:45,707
Push a magnet into a coil slowly,

64
00:02:45,727 --> 00:02:47,840
and the needle barely stirs.

65
00:02:47,860 --> 00:02:50,360
Push it in fast, and it jumps.

66
00:02:50,380 --> 00:02:52,480
The total change is the same.

67
00:02:52,500 --> 00:02:54,480
The speed is not.

68
00:02:54,500 --> 00:02:57,880
Engineers call this voltage an electromotive force,

69
00:02:57,900 --> 00:02:59,600
or EMF.

70
00:02:59,620 --> 00:03:02,890
It is not a force at all, but the old name has stuck.

71
00:03:02,980 --> 00:03:07,130
It means the voltage that drives current round a circuit.

72
00:03:07,380 --> 00:03:09,130
There is one more thing you can do.

73
00:03:09,540 --> 00:03:12,210
Use a coil instead of a single loop.

74
00:03:12,340 --> 00:03:15,480
Every turn has the same flux changing through it,

75
00:03:15,500 --> 00:03:18,000
so every turn gets the same voltage,

76
00:03:18,020 --> 00:03:19,450
and they add.

77
00:03:19,540 --> 00:03:22,650
A hundred turns give a hundred times the voltage.

78
00:03:22,980 --> 00:03:27,600
So Faraday's law says: the EMF equals the number of turns,

79
00:03:27,620 --> 00:03:29,930
times the rate of change of flux.

80
00:03:31,300 --> 00:03:33,530
Which way does the induced current flow?

81
00:03:34,020 --> 00:03:37,010
We met the answer in the video on inductance.

82
00:03:37,060 --> 00:03:41,520
It is Lenz's law: the induced current always flows so

83
00:03:41,540 --> 00:03:44,130
as to oppose the change that caused it.

84
00:03:44,260 --> 00:03:46,970
Push the north pole of a magnet toward a loop.

85
00:03:47,380 --> 00:03:50,440
The current that flows turns the near face of the loop

86
00:03:50,460 --> 00:03:54,080
into a north pole as well, so the loop pushes back on

87
00:03:54,100 --> 00:03:55,050
the magnet.

88
00:03:55,140 --> 00:03:58,010
Pull the magnet away, and the current reverses.

89
00:03:58,420 --> 00:04:00,680
Now the loop attracts the magnet,

90
00:04:00,700 --> 00:04:02,250
trying to hold on to it.

91
00:04:02,340 --> 00:04:04,650
Either way, you feel resistance.

92
00:04:04,740 --> 00:04:07,410
You have to do work to move the magnet.

93
00:04:07,460 --> 00:04:10,720
And that is exactly where the electrical energy comes

94
00:04:10,740 --> 00:04:11,410
from.

95
00:04:11,540 --> 00:04:15,250
The work your hand does becomes the energy of the current.

96
00:04:15,380 --> 00:04:17,210
Suppose it went the other way.

97
00:04:17,300 --> 00:04:20,650
Suppose the induced current helped the magnet along.

98
00:04:20,740 --> 00:04:22,840
Then the magnet would speed up,

99
00:04:22,860 --> 00:04:25,360
induce more current, and speed up again,

100
00:04:25,380 --> 00:04:28,490
for ever, giving out energy from nothing.

101
00:04:28,580 --> 00:04:31,920
Lenz's law is simply the conservation of energy,

102
00:04:31,940 --> 00:04:34,210
written for magnets and wires.

103
00:04:34,340 --> 00:04:37,280
You can see it with a strong magnet and a thick copper

104
00:04:37,300 --> 00:04:37,850
pipe.

105
00:04:38,180 --> 00:04:39,930
Copper is not magnetic.

106
00:04:40,260 --> 00:04:42,520
Yet drop the magnet down the pipe,

107
00:04:42,540 --> 00:04:44,010
and it does not fall.

108
00:04:44,100 --> 00:04:47,450
It drifts down, slowly, as if through honey.

109
00:04:47,780 --> 00:04:50,840
The falling magnet changes the flux through each ring

110
00:04:50,860 --> 00:04:51,610
of the pipe.

111
00:04:51,940 --> 00:04:55,290
Currents flow in the copper, and they oppose the fall.

112
00:04:56,667 --> 00:04:59,047
There is a second way to look at all this,

113
00:04:59,067 --> 00:05:02,007
and it ties back to something we already know.

114
00:05:02,027 --> 00:05:05,207
Take a straight piece of wire and move it sideways through

115
00:05:05,227 --> 00:05:06,807
a magnetic field.

116
00:05:06,827 --> 00:05:09,234
The wire is full of free electrons,

117
00:05:09,254 --> 00:05:11,554
and they are being carried along with it.

118
00:05:11,574 --> 00:05:14,674
So they are charges moving through a magnetic field.

119
00:05:14,694 --> 00:05:17,887
And in the video on magnetism, we saw that such a charge

120
00:05:17,907 --> 00:05:21,247
feels a force, at right angles to its motion.

121
00:05:21,267 --> 00:05:23,980
That force pushes the electrons along the wire,

122
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:25,447
toward one end.

123
00:05:25,467 --> 00:05:28,727
One end becomes negative, the other positive.

124
00:05:28,747 --> 00:05:31,407
A voltage has appeared across the wire.

125
00:05:31,427 --> 00:05:33,447
Its size is easy to remember.

126
00:05:33,467 --> 00:05:36,300
It is the field, times the length of the wire,

127
00:05:36,320 --> 00:05:37,607
times the speed.

128
00:05:37,627 --> 00:05:41,447
This is induction by motion, and it is how Faraday built

129
00:05:41,467 --> 00:05:43,047
the first generator.

130
00:05:43,067 --> 00:05:45,154
In October of eighteen thirty-one,

131
00:05:45,174 --> 00:05:48,407
he mounted a copper disc so that its edge turned between

132
00:05:48,427 --> 00:05:50,527
the poles of a large magnet.

133
00:05:50,547 --> 00:05:53,927
One contact rubbed on the rim, another on the axle.

134
00:05:53,947 --> 00:05:57,127
When he turned the handle, a steady current flowed.

135
00:05:57,147 --> 00:05:58,247
It was feeble.

136
00:05:58,267 --> 00:06:01,887
But for the first time, motion was being turned directly

137
00:06:01,907 --> 00:06:04,167
into a continuous electric current.

138
00:06:04,187 --> 00:06:07,527
The same thing happens on a grand scale every day.

139
00:06:07,547 --> 00:06:10,700
An airliner is a metal bar forty metres long,

140
00:06:10,720 --> 00:06:13,847
flying at high speed through the Earth's magnetic field.

141
00:06:13,867 --> 00:06:16,487
Between its wingtips there is a voltage:

142
00:06:16,507 --> 00:06:19,217
only a fraction of a volt, but real.

143
00:06:20,567 --> 00:06:22,277
Now make it practical.

144
00:06:22,407 --> 00:06:25,907
Take a loop of wire, and spin it steadily in a magnetic

145
00:06:25,927 --> 00:06:26,717
field.

146
00:06:26,887 --> 00:06:31,077
As the loop turns, the flux through it rises and falls.

147
00:06:31,207 --> 00:06:33,227
When it faces the field squarely,

148
00:06:33,247 --> 00:06:34,997
the flux is greatest.

149
00:06:35,127 --> 00:06:37,547
A quarter turn later, it is edge-on,

150
00:06:37,567 --> 00:06:39,037
and the flux is zero.

151
00:06:39,527 --> 00:06:42,307
Another quarter turn, and the field passes through from

152
00:06:42,327 --> 00:06:43,277
the other side.

153
00:06:43,687 --> 00:06:47,427
But remember, the voltage depends on how fast the flux

154
00:06:47,447 --> 00:06:50,157
is changing, not on how large it is.

155
00:06:50,647 --> 00:06:53,987
When the loop is face-on, the flux is at its peak,

156
00:06:54,007 --> 00:06:57,077
and for that instant it is hardly changing at all.

157
00:06:57,127 --> 00:06:58,797
So the voltage is zero.

158
00:06:59,287 --> 00:07:02,227
When the loop is edge-on, the flux is passing through

159
00:07:02,247 --> 00:07:05,277
zero and changing as fast as it ever does.

160
00:07:05,447 --> 00:07:07,597
So the voltage is at its greatest.

161
00:07:08,167 --> 00:07:11,587
Keep turning, and the voltage swings smoothly one way,

162
00:07:11,607 --> 00:07:14,317
then the other, once in every revolution.

163
00:07:14,807 --> 00:07:16,717
That is alternating current.

164
00:07:17,287 --> 00:07:21,107
The mains supply is a sine wave for one simple reason:

165
00:07:21,127 --> 00:07:23,837
it is made by something going round.

166
00:07:24,007 --> 00:07:26,147
Spin the loop fifty times a second,

167
00:07:26,167 --> 00:07:28,317
and you get fifty cycles a second.

168
00:07:28,727 --> 00:07:31,797
That is where the frequency of the grid comes from.

169
00:07:32,007 --> 00:07:35,507
The first machine of this kind was built in Paris in eighteen

170
00:07:35,527 --> 00:07:39,427
thirty-two, by an instrument maker named Hippolyte Pixii,

171
00:07:39,447 --> 00:07:41,837
a few months after Faraday published.

172
00:07:41,927 --> 00:07:43,837
It was turned by hand.

173
00:07:44,007 --> 00:07:47,347
A modern generator in a power station works in exactly

174
00:07:47,367 --> 00:07:48,427
the same way.

175
00:07:48,447 --> 00:07:52,557
It is simply larger, by a factor of about a hundred million.

176
00:07:53,900 --> 00:07:57,840
Faraday's ring, with its two coils on one iron core,

177
00:07:57,860 --> 00:07:59,570
was not just an experiment.

178
00:07:59,900 --> 00:08:02,370
It was the first transformer.

179
00:08:02,540 --> 00:08:05,170
Put an alternating current through the first coil.

180
00:08:05,500 --> 00:08:08,370
It makes an alternating flux in the iron.

181
00:08:08,540 --> 00:08:11,640
That same flux passes through the second coil,

182
00:08:11,660 --> 00:08:13,720
and because it is always changing,

183
00:08:13,740 --> 00:08:15,890
it induces a voltage there.

184
00:08:16,060 --> 00:08:19,240
Each turn of the second coil gets the same voltage as

185
00:08:19,260 --> 00:08:22,840
each turn of the first, because the same flux passes through

186
00:08:22,860 --> 00:08:23,680
both.

187
00:08:23,700 --> 00:08:27,320
So the ratio of the voltages is simply the ratio of the

188
00:08:27,340 --> 00:08:28,250
turns.

189
00:08:28,380 --> 00:08:31,480
Twice as many turns on the second coil gives twice the

190
00:08:31,500 --> 00:08:32,370
voltage.

191
00:08:32,460 --> 00:08:34,490
Half as many gives half.

192
00:08:34,620 --> 00:08:37,040
You do not get something for nothing.

193
00:08:37,060 --> 00:08:40,360
The power going in equals the power coming out,

194
00:08:40,380 --> 00:08:42,120
apart from small losses.

195
00:08:42,140 --> 00:08:44,840
So if the voltage goes up ten times,

196
00:08:44,860 --> 00:08:47,360
the current comes down ten times.

197
00:08:47,380 --> 00:08:50,680
And that is the reason the whole world uses alternating

198
00:08:50,700 --> 00:08:51,560
current.

199
00:08:51,580 --> 00:08:54,520
The heat wasted in a transmission line depends on the

200
00:08:54,540 --> 00:08:55,730
square of the current.

201
00:08:56,060 --> 00:08:58,920
Send the same power at ten times the voltage,

202
00:08:58,940 --> 00:09:02,800
and the current is a tenth, and the loss is a hundredth.

203
00:09:02,820 --> 00:09:05,600
So we generate at a moderate voltage,

204
00:09:05,620 --> 00:09:08,520
step it up to hundreds of thousands of volts for the long

205
00:09:08,540 --> 00:09:10,680
journey, and step it down again,

206
00:09:10,700 --> 00:09:13,650
in stages, to something safe for a house.

207
00:09:13,740 --> 00:09:16,770
A transformer cannot do this with direct current.

208
00:09:17,020 --> 00:09:19,400
A steady current gives a steady flux,

209
00:09:19,420 --> 00:09:22,050
and a steady flux induces nothing.

210
00:09:22,140 --> 00:09:25,680
That one fact decided the great argument of the eighteen

211
00:09:25,700 --> 00:09:28,730
eighties between direct and alternating current.

212
00:09:28,780 --> 00:09:31,880
Practical transformers were built in Budapest in eighteen

213
00:09:31,900 --> 00:09:35,160
eighty-five, and in the United States by William Stanley

214
00:09:35,180 --> 00:09:36,370
the year after.

215
00:09:36,460 --> 00:09:39,960
By eighteen ninety-five, the falls at Niagara were driving

216
00:09:39,980 --> 00:09:41,960
alternating current generators,

217
00:09:41,980 --> 00:09:45,730
and the pattern of every power system since was set.

218
00:09:47,100 --> 00:09:50,210
Induction does not need a neat coil of wire.

219
00:09:50,300 --> 00:09:51,810
Any piece of metal will do.

220
00:09:52,300 --> 00:09:55,400
Swing a solid copper plate through the gap of a strong

221
00:09:55,420 --> 00:09:56,330
magnet.

222
00:09:56,460 --> 00:09:59,960
As it enters, the flux through the metal changes,

223
00:09:59,980 --> 00:10:02,920
and currents swirl inside the plate itself,

224
00:10:02,940 --> 00:10:04,930
like whirlpools in a stream.

225
00:10:05,100 --> 00:10:07,960
They are called eddy currents, and they were studied by

226
00:10:07,980 --> 00:10:11,000
Léon Foucault in eighteen fifty-five.

227
00:10:11,020 --> 00:10:13,810
By Lenz's law, they oppose the motion.

228
00:10:13,900 --> 00:10:17,250
The plate stops as if it had hit something soft.

229
00:10:17,420 --> 00:10:20,290
Sometimes that is exactly what we want.

230
00:10:20,460 --> 00:10:24,120
Roller coasters and high-speed trains use it for braking:

231
00:10:24,140 --> 00:10:27,090
there is nothing to wear out, because nothing touches.

232
00:10:27,500 --> 00:10:30,850
An induction cooker uses eddy currents to heat the pan.

233
00:10:31,180 --> 00:10:34,280
A metal detector finds a coin by the eddy currents it

234
00:10:34,300 --> 00:10:35,250
sets up in it.

235
00:10:35,740 --> 00:10:37,970
And sometimes it is a nuisance.

236
00:10:38,380 --> 00:10:41,280
The iron core of a transformer is metal too,

237
00:10:41,300 --> 00:10:43,090
sitting in a changing flux.

238
00:10:43,420 --> 00:10:46,440
If it were solid, eddy currents would swirl through it

239
00:10:46,460 --> 00:10:48,530
and waste energy as heat.

240
00:10:48,700 --> 00:10:52,240
So the core is built from thin sheets of steel,

241
00:10:52,260 --> 00:10:54,250
each insulated from the next.

242
00:10:54,380 --> 00:10:57,080
The currents are confined to narrow slices,

243
00:10:57,100 --> 00:10:59,730
and the loss falls to a small fraction.

244
00:11:01,100 --> 00:11:03,880
A motor and a generator are the same machine.

245
00:11:03,900 --> 00:11:07,960
Push current through the loop, and the field turns it:

246
00:11:07,980 --> 00:11:09,410
that is a motor.

247
00:11:09,580 --> 00:11:12,920
Turn the loop by hand, and it gives out current:

248
00:11:12,940 --> 00:11:14,290
that is a generator.

249
00:11:14,700 --> 00:11:17,930
In fact, it is always both at once.

250
00:11:18,140 --> 00:11:21,720
As a motor spins, its coils are turning in a magnetic

251
00:11:21,740 --> 00:11:24,850
field, so a voltage is induced in them.

252
00:11:25,340 --> 00:11:28,130
By Lenz's law, it opposes the supply.

253
00:11:28,460 --> 00:11:32,490
It is called the back EMF, and it grows with speed.

254
00:11:32,700 --> 00:11:35,890
This explains something every engineer has to know.

255
00:11:36,060 --> 00:11:40,440
At full speed, the back EMF nearly balances the supply,

256
00:11:40,460 --> 00:11:43,560
and the motor draws only a small current.

257
00:11:43,580 --> 00:11:47,560
But at the moment of starting, or if the shaft is jammed,

258
00:11:47,580 --> 00:11:50,040
there is no back EMF at all.

259
00:11:50,060 --> 00:11:53,680
The current is limited only by the resistance of the windings,

260
00:11:53,700 --> 00:11:56,600
and it can be many times the normal value.

261
00:11:56,620 --> 00:11:59,440
That is why large motors need starters,

262
00:11:59,460 --> 00:12:02,280
and why a stalled motor burns out.

263
00:12:02,300 --> 00:12:04,440
It also works in your favour.

264
00:12:04,460 --> 00:12:07,360
When an electric car or train slows down,

265
00:12:07,380 --> 00:12:10,040
its motor is being driven by the wheels.

266
00:12:10,060 --> 00:12:13,720
It becomes a generator, and sends energy back to the battery

267
00:12:13,740 --> 00:12:14,960
or the line.

268
00:12:14,980 --> 00:12:18,360
And the inductance we met two videos ago is the same law

269
00:12:18,380 --> 00:12:19,210
again.

270
00:12:19,340 --> 00:12:21,810
A coil sits in its own flux.

271
00:12:21,900 --> 00:12:24,680
Change its current, and you change that flux,

272
00:12:24,700 --> 00:12:27,810
and the coil induces a voltage in itself.

273
00:12:29,167 --> 00:12:30,677
So let's put it together.

274
00:12:31,007 --> 00:12:34,107
Magnetic flux is the amount of field passing through a

275
00:12:34,127 --> 00:12:34,677
loop.

276
00:12:35,007 --> 00:12:38,667
Whenever the flux changes, a voltage is induced,

277
00:12:38,687 --> 00:12:41,957
equal to the number of turns times the rate of change.

278
00:12:42,047 --> 00:12:43,637
That is Faraday's law.

279
00:12:43,967 --> 00:12:46,997
The induced current always opposes the change.

280
00:12:47,167 --> 00:12:51,397
That is Lenz's law, and it is the conservation of energy.

281
00:12:51,727 --> 00:12:55,067
Spin a coil in a field, and you have a generator,

282
00:12:55,087 --> 00:12:56,757
and alternating current.

283
00:12:56,847 --> 00:12:59,227
Link two coils with a changing flux,

284
00:12:59,247 --> 00:13:00,837
and you have a transformer.

285
00:13:01,087 --> 00:13:03,877
And that completes the picture we set out to draw.

286
00:13:04,207 --> 00:13:06,957
Charge, and the electric field around it.

287
00:13:07,007 --> 00:13:10,347
Resistance, as that charge struggles through a wire.

288
00:13:10,367 --> 00:13:13,837
Capacitance, storing energy in an electric field.

289
00:13:13,887 --> 00:13:16,907
Magnetism, the field of charge in motion.

290
00:13:16,927 --> 00:13:20,277
Inductance, storing energy in a magnetic field.

291
00:13:20,447 --> 00:13:23,307
The two fields, bound together as one.

292
00:13:23,327 --> 00:13:27,387
And induction, which turns motion into electricity and

293
00:13:27,407 --> 00:13:28,637
powers the world.

294
00:13:28,687 --> 00:13:32,437
Every circuit you will ever meet is built from these ideas.

295
00:13:32,607 --> 00:13:34,357
Thank you for following the series.

296
00:13:34,527 --> 00:13:37,787
If this helped you see it, and not just calculate it,

297
00:13:37,807 --> 00:13:40,837
please like, share, and subscribe to this channel.
